# Rebuild and restart after a source change

After changing anything under `Sources/`, rebuild the app bundle. Verify its UI
in the test VM so testing leaves the user's Mac available.

```bash
zsh Scripts/build-app.zsh debug
testvm note 'Checking the Work Tracebook change'
testvm open ".build/Work Tracebook.app"
testvm ui "Work Tracebook"
testvm shot "Work Tracebook"
```

After the VM check passes, gracefully quit the local copy and open the new build.
Launch it without test data environment variables.

```bash
osascript -e 'quit app "Work Tracebook"'
while pgrep -x 'Work Tracebook' >/dev/null; do sleep 0.5; done
env -u FACTORYLOG_EVENTS_FILE -u FACTORYLOG_STORE open ".build/Work Tracebook.app"
```

The shipped executable is `Contents/MacOS/Work Tracebook`. The SwiftPM product
remains `FactoryLogApp`; launch the `.app` bundle, which has the Info.plist and
code signature.

# Demo screenshots

Debug builds listen for `dev.factorylog.debug.snapshot`. Send a distributed
notification inside the VM with an object of the form `screen|output-path`.
Screens are `today`, `yesterday`, `week` and `insights`, optionally followed by
`@<project path>` to open a project panel. An empty screen keeps the current view.

```bash
testvm run 'swift -e '\''import Foundation; DistributedNotificationCenter.default().postNotificationName(.init("dev.factorylog.debug.snapshot"), object: "week|/tmp/week.png", userInfo: nil, deliverImmediately: true)'\'''
testvm pull /tmp/week.png /tmp/work-tracebook-week.png
```

Launch with `-DebugActiveWindow YES` for publishable captures, and use a generated
demo log from `Scripts/make-demo-log.py`. The snapshot hook exists only in debug
builds. Keep the bundle ID `dev.factorylog.app` and its defaults domain, which
holds the user's preferences.
